<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indo-Pakistani War of 1965</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Second Kashmir War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indo-Pakistani War of 1965 was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> from August to September 1965. It began with Operation <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>, an attempt by Pakistan to infiltrate fighters into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, and widened into large tank battles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> ceasefire ended the fighting, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent-declaration">Tashkent Declaration</a> of January 1966 restored the prewar positions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indo-pakistani-war-of-1965.jpg" alt="Indo-Pakistani War of 1965" width="320" height="204" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abhinayrathore at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1965_Indo-Pak_War_DestroyedShermanTank.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Since the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> in August 1947 the two states had contended over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> and over other borders, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rann-of-kutch">Rann of Kutch</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, a dispute that arose in 1956 and ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> regaining control. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> had signed a defence agreement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1954 and in the 1960s received 700 million dollars of military aid. After India's defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-indian-war">Sino-Indian War</a> of 1962, its army was being reorganised and re-equipped. Pakistan's forces were smaller but had an edge in air power and armour, which Pakistan wished to use before India completed its build-up.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 5 August 1965 Pakistani soldiers dressed as Kashmiri locals crossed the Line of Control in Operation Gibraltar, intending to start an insurgency. Indian forces, tipped off by local people, captured several of them, and the operation failed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> took the Haji Pir pass, 8 km inside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> administered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, by 28 August. On 1 September Pakistan launched Operation Grand Slam to capture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhnoor">Akhnoor</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and cut Indian supply lines. On 6 September India crossed the international border in the west, and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayub-khan">Ayub Khan</a> declared a state of emergency, proclaiming that Pakistan was at war.</p><p>The fighting included large armoured battles. In the Sialkot sector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s 1st Armoured Division met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>'s 6th Armoured Division at Phillora after 9 September, and Pakistan lost 66 tanks to India's 6 before falling back on Chawinda. At Asal Uttar, Pakistan's 1st Armoured Division pushed towards Khem Karan to threaten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, but India breached the Madhupur canal on 11 September and the offensive was halted. The war was a stalemate, with both sides holding the other's territory. About 3,000 Indian and 3,800 Pakistani soldiers were killed. The air forces of both countries fought each other for the first time since independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 20 September the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a> passed a resolution demanding an unconditional ceasefire within 48 hours. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> accepted at once and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> on 23 September. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> under Premier Alexei Kosygin hosted talks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>, where Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lal-bahadur-shastri">Lal Bahadur Shastri</a> and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayub-khan">Ayub Khan</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent-declaration">Tashkent Declaration</a> on 10 January 1966, agreeing to withdraw to the lines held before August by 25 February 1966. Shastri died of a heart attack on 11 January.</p><p>Many Pakistanis criticised the ceasefire, having been told in official reports that the army was doing well, and student riots followed. In India Shastri was hailed as a national hero, and the outcome was widely viewed as a political and strategic victory, given the failure of the insurgency in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. Pakistan's forces, in this view, had to turn to the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “India–Pakistan war of 1965” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
